  "narrative": {
    "intro": "South Dakota received $780M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The federal government issued 3,457 awards in the state, with an average award size of $226K. Obligations fell 3.0% year over year. The Department of Defense obligated $338M and the Department of Health and Human Services obligated $135M, together accounting for a large share of the FY2026 to date total. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry by obligations at $211M, followed by Computer Facilities Management Services at $71.2M. CONTI FEDERAL SERVICES, LLC is the largest vendor in the state with $86.6M in obligations, followed by KBR WYLE SERVICES, LLC at $70.9M. Recent fiscal years show higher peaks: FY24 recorded $1.3B and FY22 recorded $1.2B in obligations, while FY25 was $804M and FY21 was $624M. The FY2026 to date picture reflects ongoing federal construction, IT services, and health-related spending, concentrated among a small set of agencies and vendors.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest federal buyers in South Dakota?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $338M in FY2026 to date. The Department of Health and Human Services obligated $135M and the Department of the Interior obligated $115M. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $102M and remaining top buyers include the Department of Homeland Security at $21.9M and the Department of Agriculture at $18.6M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which vendors and industries get the most federal dollars in South Dakota?",
        "body": "CONTI FEDERAL SERVICES, LLC received $86.6M and KBR WYLE SERVICES, LLC received $70.9M in FY2026 to date. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the top industry with $211M in obligations, and Computer Facilities Management Services is the next-largest industry with $71.2M. Other notable vendors include STERLING COMPUTERS CORPORATION at $50.1M and BLACK HILLS SERVICE COMPANY, LLC at $42.8M."
      }
    ]
